Anthony J. Stuckey from University of Illinois at Urbana writes:

>To: nethack-bugs@linc.cis.upenn.edu
>Subject: possible bug w/ cursed items, some comments.

>Now on to the comments.
>	This entire line of sight algorithm thing should
>be taken out back and shot.


It does seem very wrong sometimes.  The player is usually given too much
of a view.  For example, when a room is glimpsed at the end of a hallway
for the first time, often *most* of it is revealed.  This seems to
happen more when there are two entrances to a room on the same side.
But usually one of these entrances is blocked by passage walls, nestled
at the end after a couple of turns.  Doesn't seem right.


>	When reading an identify scroll, I was prompted
>for what I wished to identify: ([!+=?)... a).
>Hitting "a" re-identified my athame.  brilliant way to waste a
>scroll.  This is absolutely _NOT_ what I would expect "a" to do.
>It should wander through the list, asking me items as it comes to them,
>or else just wander through the list identifying things that aren't.


"a" in the menu always means "do all items, starting with the lowest-
lettered.  This is why it did your athame.  The "A" menu option is the
one meaning "query on all items".  You're right, the identify menu
should have an "A" option.  All of these menus should be consistent.

I would also like to see the following change:  when a menu item
is selected that brings up a long list of items, such as "*"
in the 't'hrow menu, allow a way to break out of the list WITHOUT
breaking out of the command entirely.  With a 4-screen inventory,
looking for a slot letter on the second screen shouldn't necessitate
paging through the rest of the inventory (awful on slow connections),
or breaking out of the command and starting completely over.  I
use ESCAPE, and that's how that behaves.

-jim


